for decades intel was the king of  semiconductors their performance was  unmatched and consumers were proud to  have an intel powered machine  even five years ago intel maintained  much of the same dominance as they  simply had no competition but with time  it seems like intel brand power and  market share are just being eroded away  year after year  meanwhile their competitors are doing  better than ever before  amd has been consistently posting  all-time high market share and nvidia  nearly reached a trillion dollar  evaluation not to mention it we've been  experiencing the biggest chip shortage  of all time over the past two years so  if intel was ever going to get back on  track it was during the past two years  but into has been struggling to even  fully recover  in fact intel is still down 37 from the  dot-com high which was set 22 years ago  in that same time period amd has grown  3x and nvidia has grown 70x  and if you needed any more confirmation  that investors have no faith in intel  you just have to take one look at their  p ratio which currently sits at 7.35  for perspective even toyota has a pe  ratio above 10 and toyota isn't the  slow-moving automotive industry not the  semiconductor industry so what happened  to intel  one of intel's first missteps was  missing out on the mobile market in the  late 2000s and early 2010s smartphones  were just starting to gain popularity  and contrary to popular belief intel was  actually on top of this  in fact they started development of a  chip called the atom in 2004 which was  specifically designed for small internet  connected devices  and the first adam chip called the  silver throne came out soon after the  first iphone in 2008 but while intel did  realize the potential of small devices  they vastly underestimated the potential  of smartphones  you see it when apple was developing the  iphone they actually approached intel  and asked them to be their chip provider  intel would spend some time doing volume  cost and revenue projections but  unfortunately intel would turn down  apple thinking that producing chips for  the iphone wouldn't be worth the effort  clearly intel vastly underestimated the  popularity of the iphone and intel's  former ceo paul otelini was actually  super upfront about the shortcoming he  stated quote in hindsight the forecasted  cost was wrong and the volume was 100x  what anyone thought  while it's great that paul was able to  acknowledge their shortcoming this  doesn't change the fact that intel not  only missed out on apple but all of the  major smartphone manufacturers from  samsung and xiaomi to oppo and huawei  and this gave qualcomm the perfect  opportunity to step in it and dominate  the sector intel would eventually come  around to this market and they would get  the adam chip into smartphones even from  samsung and the lg but intel never  became the primary chip provider for any  of these companies not just because they  were late but because they didn't  produce arm architecture chips  if you're not familiar with or market  your chips these are basically the  standard within the mobile industry the  key difference between arm chips and  intel chips is their power draw arm  chips are specifically designed to be as  power efficient as possible while intel  chips are generally optimized to offer  as much performance as possible intel  took a gamble with performance over  efficiency and this was definitely the  wrong move given that 95 smartphones are  powered with arm-based chips today intel  tried to hang on as long as possible but  they would eventually give up in 2016  after losing a major contract  while intel had decisively lost out on  the smartphone chip market they did spot  one more opportunity within the mobile  market and that was 5g modems  intel was actually rather early to this  market and they were even in talks with  apple to become their primary 5g modem  producer  but the problem was that apple was not  entertaining intel's offer because intel  was their top choice but because they  had issues with qualcomm and  unfortunately for intel apple and  qualcomm sell their differences right  before intel's partnership was about to  begin  intel could have tried to compete  against qualcomm but they didn't really  want to do this so they would end up  selling their 5g modem business to apple  for 1 billion dollars and before you  knew it into had missed out on their  first wave of smartphones the second  wave of smartphones and the race towards  5g  aside from missing out big on the  smartphone market intel would also face  serious competition within their core  market from none other than amd  this was definitely something that intel  did not see coming from their  perspective they had won the desktop  market they had loads of deals all over  the place even including apple but this  would all change when a woman named lisa  sue joined amd in 2012. lisa knew that  intel had a massive lead in terms of  technology not to mention amd was nearly  bankrupt so the only way amd could catch  up was by risking it all and that's  exactly what she did  one of the core characteristics of all  semiconductor companies is the tick tock  model  this is when companies switch between  improving the chip itself to improving  the manufacturing process  improvements to both of these are  crucial in producing more powerful chips  but companies will never attempt to do  both of them at the same time  doing both at the same time makes  troubleshooting errors extremely  difficult because the error could be due  to the new manufacturing process or due  to the new chip despite this lisa chose  to ditch the tick tock model and just  try to improve both sides at the same  time  for years we had little idea as to how  well this process was going but when  ryzen and epic were launched in 2017 it  was clear that amd had hit it out of the  park amd had not only caught up the  intel in terms of technology but they  had actually outdone them especially in  terms of multitasking for the same price  ryzen offered significantly more cores  and threads than intel now of course  each of these cores were less powerful  than intel's but for many tasks to  streaming having more cores was  preferable to having fewer more powerful  cores and amd was just getting started  lisa's goal wasn't just to beat intel  but to leave them in the dust and that's  exactly what they've done  one of the clear signs of this was how  quickly amd was able to transition to  the next architecture  if you're not familiar with chips  they're usually classified by a size  such as 7 nanometers or 10 nanometers  which is indicative of their fabrication  process  the long-term goal is to make these  chips as small as possible and let's  just say amd smoked intel to 10  nanometer chips amd launched their seven  nanometer architecture 3000 series chips  in mid-2019 meanwhile intel didn't  complete their transition to 10  nanometers till early 2021. now it  should be noted that amd7 nanometer  architecture is roughly equivalent to  intel's 10 nanometer architecture but  this still means that amd beat intel to  market by roughly 18 months  all of this has made amd the top choice  for desktop users in fact amd overtook  intel in terms of desktop cpu market  share in early 2021 and at this point  the only reason intel is still hanging  on is because they have so many deals  with laptop and pc makers and gamers  still often prefer intel due to superior  single core performance but if intel  doesn't make moves against amd right now  it's likely that this market will slowly  dissipate as well  the rise of the mobile market and stiff  competition from amd were no doubt major  external threats to intel's business but  honestly amd likely wouldn't have been  able to catch up and even humiliate  intel if intel was on top of their game  i mean if you want proof just take a  look at the gpu market lisa has advanced  amd exponentially within the gpu space  as well but they still can't quite catch  up to nvidia now don't get me wrong here  amd gpus have become a solid option  especially from a price to performance  perspective however if we simply take a  look at the most powerful gpus on the  market there's no question that nvidia  is the clear winner and the key reason  for this is that nvidia and jensen huang  were always on their a-game so when amd  started throwing punches with the radeon  and vega gpus nvidia was well prepared  to fight back meanwhile intel was simply  caught off guard and one of the main  reasons for this is intel's leadership  crisis intel's leadership issues can be  traced back to 2005 when paul ortolini  took over paul was actually the first  ceo of intellect that was not an  engineer he had joined the company sales  and marketing division in 1974 and he  slowly worked his way up from there  paul's lacking engineering background  was actually a major concern when he  took over but he appeased much of the  skeptics with phenomenal revenue paul  was a master marketer and deal maker  which allowed intel to become more  dominant than ever before within a  desktop market with that being said  though it could be argued that paul's  lack of engineering experience resulted  in him making poor fundamental decisions  for the company for example he did not  embrace arm-based chips i feel that  someone with a technological background  like jensen wong or lisa sue would have  made this jump in a heartbeat but while  paul may have made some bad  technological moves at least he blew up  into his profits and revenue intel's  next ceo brian krasnich didn't do this  either  intel's revenue basically just went  sideways throughout all of his  leadership this makes sense as most of  brian's leadership was just filled with  playing out of the smartphone market the  worst part however was the drama that  brian brought with him you see it in  2018 it would be revealed that brian was  having an affair with an employee and  this would get him fired bob swan would  replace him at seo and unfortunately for  bob this was terrible timing he  inherited into all right as amd was  doubling down on ryzen which meant that  bob had to take all the heat from  falling behind  and it was under bob's leadership that  intel struggled to transition to 10  nanometer chips to make things even  worse it was under his leadership that  apple ditched intel as you would guess  bob didn't last very long and he was  quickly replaced by patrick gelsinger in  early 2021 fortunately patrick does have  a technical background and he's been  with intel for over 40 years so  hopefully he's the right person to turn  intel around but there's no doubt that  the last decade of intel's leadership  wasn't the most productive  looking forward intel isn't no risk of  going bankrupt they're still pulling in  tens of billions in profits every single  year but with that being said intel is  not in the best of positions either  they're no longer the dominant chip  player that they used to be and looking  forward it it seems more and more likely  that intel turns into something like  atnt cisco or ge  while these companies are massive  they're not exactly exciting or  innovative and in a fast-moving industry  such as semiconductors it's not clear  how long intel can just remain dormant  the rest of the industry has already  clipped intel several fold over the past  decade nvidia is currently worth two and  a half times intel and that's with  nvidia being down 50 from their all-time  high and earlier this year even amd  overtook intel in terms of market cap  which was unthinkable just five years  ago also let's not forget about the chip  shortage amd's profits grew 5x during  the shortage qualcomm's profits grew  forex during the shortage and nvidia's  profits grew 3x during the shortage  meanwhile intel's net income didn't even  move and if intel can't catch up during  a chip shortage it's unclear if they  ever will  at this point it seems like the best  case scenario for intel is to just  maintain their market share because the  days of intel dominance are long gone do  you think intel can recover comment that  down below also drop a like if you're  glad that amd introduced competition  into the market and of course consider  checking out our international channels  to watch your videos in other languages  and consider subscribing see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hurry and i'll see you guys on  the next one
 

